#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130324 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ below. We will use this graph to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 4} \\sqrt{x} \\, .$\n\n![](images/e6/e6423b96ce60348e780ffd437207bdbeb456e3aae2888d7525f8c4e680f4ee43.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $4,$ the value of $y$ approaches $\\sqrt{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} \\sqrt{x} = \\sqrt{4} = 2.\n$$\n\nThe limit of the square root function at a point is equal to the value of the square root function at that point, and this is true for any point *that is greater than $0.$* However, for points that are less than or equal to $0,$ the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,c}\\sqrt{x}=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{c} & \\text{ if }c>0 \\\\ \\text{DNE} & \\text{ if }c\\le 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhy doesn't the limit exist for $c \\leq 0$?\n\n- For $c < 0,$ the limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, c} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because the square root function $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ is not defined for negative inputs.\n\n- Although $\\sqrt{x}$ is defined at $x=0,$ the left limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0^-} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because $\\sqrt{x}$ is undefined left of $x=0.$ Consequently, the overall limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist either." :tutorial/id #uuid "065200d4-2dec-598b-bc96-23cbca11d178" :tutorial/math-academy-id 214 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130325 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our plot of $y=\\sqrt x$ and work out its limits at infinity.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b7cbc735c20a866b1d22287e2f3a1ffc247931c2de08c545cbd2fe922397b.png)\n\nAs $x \\to \\infty,$ the square root function increases without bound. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\sqrt{x} = \\infty.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, when computing the limit as $x \\to -\\infty,$ remember that the square root function isn't defined for $x<0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty}\\sqrt{x} = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dbe32211-a583-5d8f-a17d-6df81655eadc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 216 :tutorial/title "The Limits at Infinity of the Square Root Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130326 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of the cube root function $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ below.\n\n![](images/20/20deb13b6bce6608c84db84472e19ade1d073c9562f4c414ebee36f4f9e9aebc.png)\n\nUnlike the square root function, the cube root is defined for all real $x.$ As $x$ approaches any finite number $a,$ the limit of the cube root function can be obtained by simply substituting $x=a$ into the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} \\sqrt[3]{x} = \\sqrt[3]{a}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we can see from the graph that the limits at infinity are\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x} = \\infty \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x} = -\\infty.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "34c1d017-afd0-500a-8f4b-9486d550e2e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 218 :tutorial/title "Limits of Cube Root Functions"}]}
